    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter June 16 with a high-ceiling offensive setup, headlined by the dual-threat speed of **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (80.98) and **Christian Yelich** (74.67). Both leadoff hitters benefit from favorable matchups against Feltner and Cecconi, respectively. **Pete Crow-Armstrong** specifically draws the day's optimal matchup, where a 17% boost from a soft SP_xERA (4.69) and a 14% platoon edge against a right-hander solidify his role as our primary catalyst for stolen bases. Similarly, **Kyle Schwarber** (69.09) and **Carson Benge** (67.04) provide elite efficiency in the top third of the order, leveraging favorable SP_xERA ratings and high-leverage lineup spots to maximize our run-scoring potential. \n\nWhile the top of the lineup is stacked, the most difficult decision of the morning involved the utility slot and the final bench spot. **Caleb Durbin** (44.94) remains the most intriguing name on the pine; he was pushed out of the active lineup primarily due to an imposing matchup against Cease (3.07 xERA). While **Caleb Durbin** holds significant long-term value, the optimizer prioritized the specific platoon leverage of **Chase Meidroth** (50.64) and **Casey Schmitt** (56.89), who benefit from softer landing spots against Holmes and Matthews. Elsewhere, we continue to monitor the status of **Hyeseong Kim** and **Rece Hinds**, who remain in the minors, and **Dillon Dingler** (34.07), who failed to clear the 40.0 efficiency floor required to warrant a start today.\n\nWe are operating with a relatively clean slate regarding weather, though users should maintain close watch on the **Yordan Alvarez** (60.65) start against Valdez. Despite his elite 0.452 xwOBA, the direct L/L platoon disadvantage dampens his projected floor. Otherwise, the lineup is optimized to chase stolen base opportunities via **Josh Smith**, whose dynamic platoon edge and slow opposing delivery provide a subtle but meaningful boost in our quest to dominate the category. 